  <section class="notes" aria-labelledby="account">
    <h2 id="account">What it does</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <dl class="account-list">
        <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>The same files 11ty 3.1.8 writes, byte for byte. It is checked by building test sites with both and comparing every file.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Templates</dt><dd>Liquid, Nunjucks, Markdown and HTML in any mix, with 11ty's data cascade, layouts, pagination, permalinks and collections.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Config</dt><dd><code>brrrty.config.rhai</code>, a <a href="https://rhai.rs">Rhai</a> script shaped like <code>eleventyConfig</code>: filters, collections, global data, passthrough copy, and the RSS and syntax highlighting plugins. <a href="/docs/configuration/">Reference</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Refusals</dt><dd>Where it can't match 11ty exactly, it refuses that page and says why, and builds the rest. It never writes a page that might differ. <a href="/docs/differences/">What it refuses</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Dev server</dt><dd><code>brrrty serve</code> rebuilds on every change and updates open pages in place. <a href="/docs/serve/">Details</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Early: version {{ release }}. Try it on a copy of your site and compare the output with 11ty's before you rely on it.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notes" aria-labelledby="bertie">
    <h2 id="bertie">The turtle</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Bertie is a turtle with a rocket on his shell. The rocket is the Rust. The turtle is the part that checks every byte before it leaves.</p>
      <p>Brrrty is three Rs, like "go brrr". This site is built with it, from the same repository, on every change.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

